<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1411"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Topeka_guy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Topeka_guy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, November 18, 2004 - 3:20 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hello, everyone,  <BR> <BR>I was just reading the recent update at Crime Library where it claims BTK&#39;s May 2004 letter was postmarked Thomas B. King. Thus we have official correspondace from BTK under aliases Bill Thomas Killman, Thomas B. King, and &#40;possibly?&#41; Kent T. Billingham. Unlike others around here, I totally believe BTK posted here as BTK, Kent&#39;s comment reeks of BTK &#40;in my opinion&#41;. What ashame Kent hasn&#39;t posted once more.  <BR> <BR>Also, according to the article, the chapter entitled &#34;PJ&#39;s&#34; from the same letter could refer to Professor P.J. Wyatt who studied the poem &#34;Oh, Death&#34; in her WSU class.  <BR> <BR>It also mentions a tool called the Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode Instrument used by police, unsure if this is a coincidence &#40;unlikely&#41;. This article also claims that BTK uses police jargon in his letters, something else I had not read elsewhere and I&#39;m not sure has been widely known.  <BR> <BR>Damn the Wichita Police for still withholding his writings and drawings, I am of the opinion that if they were to release more correspondances someone would eventually recognize BTK&#39;s style of writing and/or drawing technique.  <BR> <BR>Also, in one interesting sidenote, it says that the person listed on the school ID BTK sent earlier this year to police never existed and that the school logo on the ID was outdated. So where did BTK have this made? If they were to publicize this ID perhaps somebody would recoginze it.  <BR> <BR>It also says that police received a major tip in 1991 which eventually fizzled but which served to reinvigorate invesigators. Does anybody have any idea what this could be about? My personal belief is that BTK wrote to police then to let them know he is still around or alternatively wrote with information about another murder unlinked to him. Does anyone have any information about what information the police might have received in 1991 in connection with BTK?  <BR> <BR>Also, as an aside, I read elsewhere that the British Psychic said that BTK was a totally normal Joe Schmo 95% of the time and only had these &#34;dark periods&#34;. Hmm. This is totally at odds with many portraits of BTK as a loner only obsessed with killing. Frankly, if I had to choose between the psychic and Wichita&#39;s finest, I&#39;m going with the Brit. Have they circulated the drawings the psychic drew/had drawn of BTK? Personally, I&#39;m curious to see them and I don&#39;t even live in Wichita.  <BR> <BR>Comments? Reactions? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1411-->
